Your cluster is now in a much better state. Here's what I recommend before reinstalling the SR-IOV Operator.

1. Cluster health

Your ClusterOperators are healthy except for image-registry:

image-registry
Available:   True
Progressing: False
Degraded:    True
Message: Degraded: The registry is removed

This is not related to the SR-IOV issue. On many SNO/disconnected/lab environments, the internal image registry is intentionally removed or disabled. The degraded message is coming from the image pruner job and is separate from SR-IOV.

2. Clean up leftover SR-IOV resources

Although the operator is removed, you still have:

✅ CRDs

✅ SriovNetworkNodePolicy objects

✅ SriovOperatorConfig


You do not have any SriovNetworkNodeState, which is good.

Before reinstalling, I recommend deleting the old policies and operator config so the new operator starts from a clean state.

oc delete sriovnetworknodepolicy --all -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

oc delete sriovoperatorconfig default -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

Verify:

oc get sriovnetworknodepolicy -A
oc get sriovoperatorconfig -A

Both should return No resources found.

3. Reinstall the SR-IOV Operator

After reinstalling:

Wait until the CSV reaches Succeeded.

Do not create any SriovNetworkNodePolicy immediately.

First verify that the operator and all its pods are healthy.


Use:

oc get csv -n openshift-sriov-network-operator
oc get pods -n openshift-sriov-network-operator

4. Apply policies gradually

Instead of applying all 8 policies at once, create one policy first and verify:

oc get sriovnetworknodestate -A -w

It should progress like:

InProgress
↓
Succeeded

Only after the first policy succeeds should you apply the remaining policies.

Why this is important

From the earlier troubleshooting, the node got stuck during the drain/reboot workflow while multiple SR-IOV policies were being reconciled. Starting with a single policy makes it much easier to identify the exact step that causes a problem if it recurs.

If you're reinstalling from OperatorHub or using a Subscription YAML, I can also review the Subscription, OperatorGroup, and CatalogSource manifests before you install to help avoid the previous issue.
